<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeremy Bentham</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeremy Bentham was an English philosopher, jurist and social reformer regarded as the founder of modern utilitarianism, which took the greatest happiness of the greatest number as the measure of right and wrong. His An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789) was the first major book on the subject. A political radical, he advocated freedom of expression, equal rights for women and the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and of capital punishment, while dismissing natural rights as nonsense upon stilts. His students included James Mill and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>, and after his death in 1832 his body was preserved as an auto-icon, now on display at University College <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham.jpg" alt="Jeremy Bentham" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry William Pickersgill · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeremy_Bentham_by_Henry_William_Pickersgill_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Romantic writers turned against what they saw as the cold rule of reason, but the Enlightenment’s ideas lived on in liberalism, in the utilitarianism of <b>Jeremy Bentham</b> and in the sciences of society.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jeremy Bentham</b> called it &quot;the war whoop of anarchy&quot; in 1796.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a></li></ul></section>
